[56.077577, "o", "The most iconic fMRI **preprocessing pipeline** paper is clearly:\r\n\r\n## 🏆 fMRIPrep — Esteban et al. (2018)\r\n> **\"fMRIPrep: a robust preprocessing pipeline for functional MRI\"**\r\n> *Nature Methods* · **3,434 citations** · DOI: [10.1038/s41592-018-0235-4](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41592-018-0235-4)\r\n\r\nThis is the field-standard, BIDS-native, reproducible preprocessing pipeline — and the one most people mean by \"iconic fMRI preprocessing.\" (Also, notably, **your own paper** — you're the first author. 😄)\r\n\r\n### Other top hits from the search\r\n| Cites | Year | Paper |\r\n|------:|------|-------|\r\n| 3,526 | 2016 | DPABI: Data Processing & Analysis for (Resting-State) Brain Imaging — *Yan et al.* |\r\n| **3,434** | **2018** | **fMRIPrep — Esteban et al.** |\r\n| 704 | 2013 | The Nuisance of Nuisance Regression — *Hallquist et al.* |\r\n| 446 | 2020 | Handbook of FC-MRI methods in CONN — *Nieto-Castanon et al.* |\r\n| 199 | 2015 | Optimization of rs-fMRI Pre-processing — *Shirer et al.* |\r\n\r\nDPABI edges it on raw citation count, but it's a broader analysis *toolbox*; fMRIPrep is the canonical **preprocessing pipeline** specifically, and the genre-defining one.\r\n\r\nWhat would you like to do next?\r\n- **Add to library** — sync one or more of these to your Zotero/`library.json`\r\n- **Refine** — e.g. restrict to a year range, or search a narrower topic (surface, denoising, motion correction)\r\n- **Just browsing** — nothing further needed\r\n"]
